Serious GitHub safety issue

Both CAD bridges execute arbitrary model-generated Python via exec(). The localhost binding helps limit network exposure, but it does not make the execution safe. copilot_flask executes generated code inside Rhino, while revit_bridge executes generated code with access to the active Revit document and API.

Before presenting this as safe or production-ready, I would label the repository clearly:

Experimental proof of concept. AI-generated code executes locally with the current user’s permissions. Review generated operations before use and test only on non-production model copies.

For the next version, add:

Preview before execution
Explicit user approval
A restricted tool allowlist
Blocked imports and file/system/network operations
Undo records or transactional rollback
Execution logs showing prompt, code, result and model
Request authentication, even on localhost

The safest long-term architecture is not unrestricted exec(). It is structured commands such as:

JSON
{
"tool": "divide_curve",
"curve_id": "...",
"segment_count": 20
}
Show more lines

Your own trusted Python implementation then validates and performs the action. The model chooses an approved operation rather than writing unrestricted machine-executable code.

